{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest","id":1199787,"title":"The Time of Salt and Iron [Quest]","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Exalted"],"posts":[{"date":"2018-03-26T12:10:38-07:00","id":1199787,"title":"The Time of Salt and Iron [Quest]","content":"The world is dying, the air filled with smoke and blood.<br /> <br /> The Shogunate is gone, the Legions withdrawn, the Dragons caught up in their ruins of empire. And, with the Dragon gone, the lesser beasts of the world have emerged to worry the edges of society.<br /> <br /> The beginning of the end was first noted in RY -393, when the barbarian peoples that lived on the eastern banks of the River of Tears <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Migration_Period\" target=\"_blank\">started migrating across</a>, into the Provinces of the East and North. Initially, this wasn\u2019t met with too much interest, in fact, such migration was allowed and even encouraged, with some Daimyo granting the barbarians land in return for service as <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foederati\" target=\"_blank\"><i>foederati</i></a> mercenaries. In but a few decades time, however, the trickle became a stream, then a flood. No longer were bands and clans crossing the limites, but entire tribes, confederations and nations. These newcomers were not content to settle on the borders, nor to peacefully integrate with the Shogunate citizen population. With the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Crisis_of_the_Third_Century\" target=\"_blank\">Interregnum in the</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sengoku_period\" target=\"_blank\">Threshold</a> <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Warring_States_period\" target=\"_blank\">coming to a boil</a>, the Legions were by-and-large unable to offer assistance, and the local <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Limitanei\" target=\"_blank\"><i>limitanei</i></a> forces could not stem the tide. As province after province fell, and more and more barbarians migrated, waves of refugees started moving into the Threshold. Those officials and commanders stationed on the borders of the Threshold provinces often noted that the barbarians that harried at the steps of the fleeing refugees seemed\u2026.. frantic. Desperate, and such desperation often manifested in brutal, sometimes-suicidal attacks in effort to win land away from the River of Tears. Contrary to \u2018popular opinion\u2019 about barbarian war tactics, this was highly unusual.<br /> <br /> As waves of migrants and war-refugees moved into the backdrop of the Interregnum, said officials soon learned why the barbarians were so desperate to move.<br /> <br /> The Great Contagion.<br /> <br /> The first outbreak was recorded as happening in <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Plague_of_Cyprian\" target=\"_blank\">RY-249</a>, in the city of Seleucia, a subsidiary suburb of Deleheshen. Within 20 years, the pandemic had spread Creation-wide, transported by the wide Shogunate trade network and aided by the ceaseless civil war and breakdown of infrastructure of the Interregnum.<br /> <br /> The horrors of the Great Contagion resonate in the souls of society even today (namely, in the public sanitation and health programs of Lookshy and the Realm), and the effects of the Contagion on society, coupled with the devastation of the Interregnum, ended in an almost-total <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Historiography_of_the_fall_of_the_Western_Roman_Empire\" target=\"_blank\">collapse of society Creation-wide</a>. There are areas of Creation where the effects of the Contagion can be felt to this day, and the catastrophe must have seemed like the end of days to those who managed to survive it. Areas with high population densities, such as urban areas and much of the East, suffered an <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Population_history_of_indigenous_peoples_of_the_Americas\" target=\"_blank\">estimated population loss of around 90%,</a> and other Directions suffered proportional dieoffs. Cities were, almost as a whole, abandoned, and these refugees from the urban centers fled to the countryside, stripping it bare and passing along not only the Great Contagion to those still unaffected, but other communicable diseases as well. Trade and accompanying infrastructure broke down, and as many people died from lack of food and clean water as they did the pandemics. Manufacturing ground to a halt as well, with goods only being produced on a local level, and themselves being cruder, a callback to an earlier age. Settlements in isolated areas tended to fare \u2018better\u2019 than those on main thoroughfares and trade routes, but this, in turn, led to outbreaks of xenophobia against refugees.<br /> <br /> The Great Contagion ravaged Creation <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Black_Death#Recurrence\" target=\"_blank\">for upwards of two centuries in some regions</a>, spreading then dying back like some living thing. Even the Exalted Dragon-Blooded, the Princes of the Earth, were affected by the disease. It is unknown if they were able to die from the disease (it is recorded that some did, in fact, become infected), but even still, the population of the Exalted host was cut in half, at the least, from the ceaseless civil wars and societal upheaval. This, in turn, left the survivors largely at the mercy of the restless dead and the Fair Folk, both of which experienced a resurgence of activity not seen again until the Fall of Thorns. Entire regions were converted to shadowlands, and entire villages were dreameaten by the rapacious Raksha, feasting off the despair of the survivors.<br /> <br /> Yet still, the survivors not only had to suffer the effects of society collapsing, coupled with the restless dead and the Fair Folk, but even the very environment seemed against them.The weather got colder and wetter, and a creeping cold swept down from the north, dropping snow on regions during late spring and early autumn, even down to the shores of the Inland Sea. The growing season was diminished, both in length and effectiveness and area, leading to regular famines and disease outbreaks resulting from malnutrition even 150 years after the first collapse. Survivors went to war, not only to defend themselves from the Dead and the Raksha, but also to take more fertile lands. The face of the Sun was hidden for weeks at a time, not only by the stormclouds, but also plumes of smoke from burning cities, and when the wind blew from the East\u2026.. it was heavy with the sickly-sweet scent of rot. It must have seemed like the very light of Creation was guttering out, and everywhere you looked was affected by Famine, Pestilence, War.<br /> <br /> Death.<br /> <br /> It is now, in this end of the world, that your story begins.<br /> <br /> Your name is Rechila, son of Hermeneric, and you are a prince of the Swadian Confederation, a group of 12 tribes sharing the same language, culture, government and gods. Twenty years ago, in RY-118, your father led the Confederation out of their near-barren homelands of the North, seeking to escape the death-throes of the inner Threshold. Instead of heading South and West, like so many other refugees, he led your people East, to the margins of the River of Tears. Still fertile, the valley of the River of Tears is all-but-empty, and could be the salvation of the 15,000 survivors you lead\u2026.. but it isn\u2019t <i>completely</i> empty. Xenophobic survivors, desperate to protect both themselves and their holdings. The Restless Dead, of the Contagion and of its aftermath, who seek to drink the blood of the living. The Fair Folk, the Cold Ones, eager to feast on the despair of your people. Cruel gods, driven to misanthropy by the death of their worshippers and a lack of a response from Heaven, will seek to prey on you in their own ways.<br /> <br /> Your father, Hermeneric, is the well-respected King of your people, having first led them from their homelands 20 years ago, but he is old, and the strain of this migration has taken its toll. He will rely on you more than ever in these coming times, and your ascension as King at the hands of the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Thing_(assembly)\" target=\"_blank\">Althing</a> is all-but guaranteed.<br /> <br /> In this time, this end of days, what sort of King will you be?<br /> <br /> If you are so interested:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Temperment: How do you present yourself to others?<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Sanguine: Associated with blood, the element of air, the season of spring and the dawn, you are extroverted, emotional and people-oriented. You tend to be charming, cheerful, passionate and expressive, but also undisciplined, disorganized, frivolous and impulsive.</li> <li>Choleric: Associated with yellow bile, the element of fire, the season of summer and the afternoon, you are extroverted, unemotional and task-oriented. You tend to be determined, confident, honest and practical, but also hot-tempered, insensitive, vindictive, and arrogant.</li> <li>Melancholic: Associated with black bile, the element of earth, the season of autumn and the evening, you are introverted, emotional and task-oriented. You tend to be dignified, detailed, thoughtful and organized, yet also critical, dogmatic, pessimistic and untrusting</li> <li>Phlegmatic: Associated with phlegm, the element of water, the season of winter and the deep night, you are introverted, unemotional and people-oriented. You tend to be polite, dependable, pleasant and perceptive, yet also indecisive, passive, stubborn and lazy.</li> <li>Eclectic: Associated with the element of wood, you are all of the above and none, changing as the mood takes you.</li> </ol></li> <li>Values: What virtue do you hold dear?<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Valor: Without bravery, what is the use?</li> <li>Compassion: All we are is how we treat others, nothing more</li> <li>Conviction: You have to see things through. Always</li> <li>Temperance: The only real luxury is the luxury of self-restraint</li> <li>Willpower: Strength, true strength, comes from within.</li> </ol></li> <li>Mother: While your father was the King of the Swadians, from a long line of nobles, your mother was:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Also a Swadian noble. Coming from a noble lineage on both sides of the family, your word carries much weight (Influence 3)</li> <li>The princess of a Shogunate successor-state. Given in treaty to ensure the then-foederati remained loyal, your mother\u2019s name may still carry weight in her homeland (Contact 1: <i>dux Gallia,</i> speak Riverspeak)</li> <li>The daughter of a chieftain of the Eridanian river-folk. The Eridanians range up and down the River of Tears, trading both materials and rumors. (Contact 3: River traders)</li> </ol></li> <li>Weapon of choice: As a Swadian warrior-noble, you are expected to be skilled at the arts of war. However, there is one weapon you are renowned for your skill with.<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Sword: the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Migration_Period_sword\" target=\"_blank\"><i>egcheru</i></a>, the Northern derivative of the Shogunate <i>spatha.</i> Broad-bladed and heavy, the <i>egcheru</i> is the traditional signifier of Northern nobility. (Melee specialty: individual combat/duels)</li> <li>Spear: the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Migration_Period_spear\" target=\"_blank\"><i>ger</i></a>, a strong-shafted spear usually about 7 feet in length, usable both on foot and on horseback, in either one or two hands. The most common weapon in the North, and arguably Creation-wide. (Melee specialty: formation fighting)</li> <li>Seax, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seax\" target=\"_blank\"><i>seax</i></a> is the knife found across almost all Northern cultures. Single-bladed with a thick back, the seax is both a useful utility tool as well as a weapon, especially in the press of a shieldwall or inside a building, and in most Northern cultures signifies freemanship. (Melee specialty: close quarters)</li> <li>Axe, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francisca\" target=\"_blank\"><i>francisca</i></a>, a light axe popular across most of the North and Northeast, suitable both for hand-to-hand combat and as a throwing weapon. The weapon has a fearsome reputation for causing damage even through armor and helmets (Melee/Thrown specialty: penetrating armor)</li> <li>The Javelin: the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Angon\" target=\"_blank\"><i>angon</i></a>, the Northern derivative of the Shogunate <i>pilum</i>, the angon is capable of punching through a wooden shield and wounding the enemy behind. In addition, the butt-end of the javelin often gets caught on the terrain, effectively removing the enemies shield from the equation even if the head doesn\u2019t penetrate. (Thrown specialty: shield disarming)</li> </ol></li> <li>Activities as a young adult: Before your current age of 26, you spend your youth and early adulthood at:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Training at war and drilling tribal levies: Part of being a ruler, even a barbarian ruler, is that you lead the people in both war and peace. You knew what your role would be, and you prepared for it. While training, you made friends with the sons of other noble families, and using your families name and resources, raised them up as your <i>beaduprat</i> (roughly, brotherhood, similar to the Realm\u2019s Sworn Brotherhood), giving you not only skill at war and arms, but a small group of warriors loyal only to you. (War 2, Melee 2, Presence 2, Command 1 [Size 1 elite battlegroup)</li> <li>Experimenting with cavalry: The Swadians, like most of the Northern peoples, lack much in the way of cavalry forces. The lack of strong, fast mounts (most Northern horses are little more than burly ponies) and a propensity for men with large frames of heavy bone and meaty muscle mean that Swadian warriors tend to function better as infantry. What little cavalry the tribes have is exceptionally light, serving mainly as scouts, screens and javelin-armed skirmishers. You, on the other hand, noticed how well the Shogunate cavalry performed, and over the years bought, bred and raised horses and trained men in their use, until you had a small force of heavy cavalry at the tribes disposal. (Ride 2, War 1, Lore: Horses 2, Backing 2)</li> <li>Playing politics in the Confederation Althing. The Althing, or the Thing of all Swadians, is the assembly of all tribal chieftains, nobles and religious leaders. While the individual clans and tribes of the Confederation have their own Things, to elect chieftains and deliberate on laws and judicial cases, the Althing decides the path of the Confederation of a whole, and much like its \u201ccivilized\u2019 counterparts in the Threshold, it is a morass of factions, backroom deals, bargaining and grandstanding. You took to the political life as soon as you became an adult at 16, not only casting your votes but also bargaining and making deals. You are confident in your ability to navigate the political parties of the Althing, and can safely say that some of the decisions reached not only ensured the Swadians continued survival, but even earned a measure of relative prosperity. (Bureaucracy 2, Integrity 2, Performance 2, Presence 2, Socialize 2)</li> <li>Received a classical education. Contrary to the popular opinion of the average Shogunate citizen concerning the \u2018barbarian peoples\u2019, your people greatly valued education, literacy and the written word. Almost every person in the Confederation is literate, in Skytongue runes at least, and you took that drive to greater heights. When you were a young child, your father took on the services of a tutor from one of the Shogunate successor-states, who taught you the languages of the Threshold, mathematics, engineering, history, medicine, and other liberal arts. As you grew older, you used your education to serve as a diplomat for the Confederation, where the recipients of the diplomatic missions were often surprised to find that the author of the well-written missives with graceful penmanship was none other than a barbarian in furs and wool. (Speak Low Realm, High Realm, Dragontongue, Lore:<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberal_arts_education\" target=\"_blank\">liberal arts</a> 1, Linguistics 2)</li> </ol></li> </ol>OOC:<br /> <br /> Welp, here is my new Quest! I am planning on updating this every Sunday, as it is when I have time off from work on my current schedule. If my schedule changes, and I get a shift of my own, I\u2019ll fill you folks in as to what will happen.<br /> <br /> If you have any questions concerning the above choices, or wish to see something different, just ask or suggest it, keeping in mind that the character is a mortal, and the culture is the equivalent of the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Migration_Period\" target=\"_blank\">Germanic peoples of the 3<sup>rd</sup>-5<sup>th</sup> centuries of Europe.</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 03-26-2018, 03:58 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-26T12:39:27-07:00","id":1199799,"title":"","content":"Oh, hey, wtf, i've been trying to post this for a day and a half already. It finally worked? Cool","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-26T12:40:06-07:00","id":1199801,"title":"","content":"- <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest#post1199787\">Characterization</a><br /> <br /> -<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1200475#post1200475\">Author's Notes: My take on the Contagion</a><br /> -<a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1202817#post1202817\">Author's Notes: Post-Contagion Trade and Economies</a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1204007#post1204007\">-Author's Notes: Northern Warfare</a><br /> <br /> -Chapter 1: The Wheel and the Road<br /> &gt; <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1202105#post1202105\">Across the River of Tears<br /> &gt;</a> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1205018#post1205018\">Shogunate successors</a>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-09-2018, 08:20 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-26T18:23:54-07:00","id":1199900,"title":"","content":"Interesting set up! So I'm clear before making my choices, will this quest be a ground level thing focusing on the travails of Rechila, or is the main focus going to be guiding Swadia using him as our conduit? So, for example, would a typical turn be closer to &quot;Will Rechila's next action be to fight or flee the foe in front of him,&quot; or more &quot;Which economic reforms will the Confederation choose next year?&quot;","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/870-side-item","name":"Side Item","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar870_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"156"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-26T18:57:25-07:00","id":1199908,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Side Item</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1199900#post1199900\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Interesting set up! So I'm clear before making my choices, will this quest be a ground level thing focusing on the travails of Rechila, or is the main focus going to be guiding Swadia using him as our conduit? So, for example, would a typical turn be closer to &quot;Will Rechila's next action be to fight or flee the foe in front of him,&quot; or more &quot;Which economic reforms will the Confederation choose next year?&quot;</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Quite a bit of both.<br /> <br /> The story will progress through Rechila's POV, but his actions and choices will have a great deal of effect on the Confederation as a whole.<br /> <br /> It is important to note that the Swadian Kingship is more of a chief executive (a decent example is the US President) than an absolute monarch. While the King is in control of the Confederation's military, as well as the chief diplomat, he can't just do what he wants. He can't declare war (although he can raise the banners and do limited military actions), he can't declare peace or trade deals (although he will certainly be the representative sent to broker/present said deals), and he can't declare hegemony over the member tribes of the Confederation (they have their own chieftains and assemblies). Basically, in order to get things done, he will have to play politics. When he becomes King, however, he will have a great deal of influence over the Althing, and have certain powers of veto.<br /> <br /> More on that when it comes up","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-27T05:11:34-07:00","id":1199986,"title":"","content":"I say, let's be Frankish invaders.<br /> <br /> Rechila is, in many respects, an exemplar of his people. He is <b>choleric </b>and sees <b>Conviction </b>as the highest virtue (with valor being a very close second). His mother is <b>the daughter of a chieftain of the Eridanian river-folk</b>. He favors the <b>francisca</b>, and spent his early life <b>training at war and drilling tribal levies</b>.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-27T11:13:16-07:00","id":1200143,"title":"","content":"I vote for Melancholic (you take duty and responsibility seriously and have devoted more energy to being the King's dependable apprentice than making friends, and your royal destiny honestly weighs heavily on you), Temperance (you know that your reputation affects your father and your house and your people, and so you're honestly a little repressed from avoiding personal weaknesses...and you have a lot of weaknesses to avoid), son of an Eridanean (and on good terms with Mom's family, since trade and numbers come easily to you and you enjoy stories of the lands up and down the river you know your responsibilities will likely keep you from seeing), an unusually skilled warrior with an axe (versatile, reliable, dependable in close quarters or at range, for splitting wood or splitting helmets, direct and a little unsubtle, much like yourself), and you've spent your early life training and building a core group of loyal warriors who know and trust you and who you can depend on to carry out your vision.<br /> <br /> I want us to honestly wish we didn't have to be King and to have some other dream we're sacrificing because of our duty to our people. Maybe some dots in Music or something?<br /> <br /> Edit: To be clear, I think we oughta be a moody brooding tragic hero hottie.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-27T13:35:35-07:00","id":1200209,"title":"","content":"To clear some things up: <br /> <br /> The Swadians (and most tribal groups in the North) do not practice primogeniture. Instead, they elect their leaders. Generally, the smaller groups elect their immediate leaders, then these leaders elect their leaders.<br /> <br /> So, in the Swadian Confederation:<br /> <br /> -Each clan elects their clan-chief<br /> -The clan-chiefs of a tribe elects their tribal chief<br /> -The Tribal chiefs elect the King<br /> <br /> Of course, the general way of things is for power to stay in the hands of established families, according to tradition. For example, the clan-chiefs of a clan are <u>almost always</u> going to be the oldest, most-well-established member of the clan. The Tribal chieftains are <u>almost always</u> going to be the most-well-established war-leader among the clan-chiefs. And, your family has held the Kingship for close to 200 years at this point.<br /> <br /> This tendency to concentrate power within families is what led to the formation of nobility amongst the Swadians (and, indeed, most of the tribal groups of the north), and the nobles keep their position through deeds, namely war, and through the giving of gifts and grants earned through those deeds.<br /> <br /> For example: a clan-chief owns all the land of a clan. In return for the continued support of their followers, the clan-chiefs divy it up to their followers, as well as giving them booty and other gifts from wars and raids. They attract an armed retinue of loyal followers who support them in war (though, well, fighting under their leadership) and in peace (by electing them clan-chief in the first place, supporting their decisions in the clan-Thing, and giving them tribute in the forms of crops or goods)<br /> <br /> When a leader dies, the clan/tribe/kingdom comes together in a Thing to elect the new chieftain. The choice of the now-deceased chief often has a great deal of influence on the final decision, but people are allowed to throw their support behind those they prefer. Prospective leaders will often try to attract new followers through promising them more land, more cattle, wealth from raids or trade, gifts of weapons and armor, the fostering of children, so on and so forth, anything that will cause the prospective votes to come their way.<br /> <br /> Generally, the tendency is for established families to keep their power, often for the sole reason that they are able to give the most gifts and fulfill the most promises to potential followers. One particularly common method of 'creating' new followers to support you is to set up one of your armed retinue as a landed tenant/clan-chief/new tribal chief.<br /> <br /> For example: Most chieftains (in Swadian Skytongue, &quot;<i>dryhten')</i> attract armed retinues, called &quot;Hirdmenn&quot; (in Swadian Skytongue, literally 'house-men, but im going to refer to them as 'retainers'), usually the younger sons of landed tenants that wouldn't have much of an inheritance. These younger sons engage in acts of bravery (raids to obtain booty, or heroics on the battlefield) in order to attract their chieftains eye, and if they get chosen, the chieftain gifts them weapons, armor, and freedom from a life of toil, in return for their 24/7 armed support as retainers (Chieftains and their Hirdmenn are semi-professional, compared to the as-needed tribal levies). The retainers live in the chieftains hall, on their dime, and when having Hirdmenn becomes too expensive, many chieftains release some retainers from their service, granting them land and status in return for political support. The retired warrior gets land and status (and therefore, more of an opportunity to start a family, and attract followers of their own), while the chieftain gets support that is loyal to him.<br /> <br /> The sons of nobility usually have the greatest impetus to go out and make names for themselves for that reason, compared to a commoners son. It isn't <u>certain</u> they will receive their parents social status, so they often feel the greatest drive to go out there, win reknown and booty, in order to attract followers and make it more likely they get elected chieftain.<br /> <br /> What does this long rambling mean?<br /> <br /> So..... that means that there is a chance, however slim, that Rechila doesn't get elected as King once his father passes or abdicates. That last bit is especially important: his father is still kicking, and will for a while. In Quest terms, it is going to be a couple chapters until it happens, and Rechila (through your decisions made) will have plenty of chances to either cement his future ascension or send it down the toilet.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-28T10:30:36-07:00","id":1200475,"title":"","content":"With regards to how i view, and will portray, the Great Contagion, since i know the question will come:<br /> <br /> I, personally, have always found the canon &quot;90% of EVERYTHING died&quot; aspect to be, honestly, vaugely ridiculous. It sounds more like something that belongs in the grimderpier editions of WH40k.<br /> <br /> Instead, i portray the Great Contagion as 'only' killing somewhere around 60% of Creation's human population. Apocalyptic, society-crumbling, yes, but Creation wouldn't be left a barren wasteland. Other animal life and plants were unharmed, by the Contagion at least.<br /> <br /> Generally, my population dieoff percentages runs like this:<br /> <br /> 1) East: 90% in the least, with some areas entirely wiped clean of human life.<br /> <br /> During 'my' Shogunate, the East, or more accurately &quot;The River Province&quot;, was the most heavily-populated area of Creation, comparable to modern-day China. Cities grew on the banks of the Yellow River and River of Tears like fleas on a dog, and even where the area wasnt urbanized, it still had re-goddamn-diculously high population densities.<br /> <br /> This meant the Great Contagion (and secondary pandemics) ripped through the region like a scythe through grain. It also meant that, once everyone was dead, there was a &quot;reverse migration&quot; of people back into the region, exactly what the Swadians are doing.<br /> <br /> 2) The Center (the Blessed Isle and the inner Threshold), about 75%, from the Great Contagion and civil war.<br /> <br /> After the River Province, the Blessed Isle and coastal inner Threshold were the most heavily urbanized areas in Creation. They too suffered from this, as well as an influx of refugees from the rest of Creation.<br /> <br /> 3) The North and the South: about 50%. Away from coastal cities, most deaths occured more due to logistical interruptions (not enough people to bring in crops, mainly) than due to sheer doompunching via Great Contagion (although the GC still hammered both Directions). The climate change also caused a lot more deaths than in other Directions, with the North getting colder and wetter (during the Shogunate, the North was capable of growing wines and other fruit. In the 'modern day, not so much, asides from apples and other hardy things), while the South got more arid, leading to the destruction of the rice industry in the region as well as the switching of barley for wheat.<br /> <br /> As an aside, the North fared better than pretty much every region except for the West, largely for the same reason: geographical isolation. <br /> <br /> 4) The West: about 30%. Then as is now, the West was the most geographically isolated region of Creation. The dieoff of trade across Creation was a mixed blessing: cant catch the Great Contagion if nobody brings it to your shores, but many islands were semi-dependent on that same trade in order to, you know.... Not die. Famines and 'lesser' diseases wiped out entire islands, and when the Great Contagion managed to get introduced, it ROFLstomped everything just like everywhere else.<br /> <br /> I also portray the Great Contagion as, well, just a regular disease, albeit a disease with a supernatural source. That means, while it is absurdly lethal, if you are fit abd healthy and have people to take care of you while you fight it off, you stand a decent chance of surviving.<br /> <br /> It is just that, well.... Not too many people in Creation had those perks. The Interregnum (the definitive Shogunate Civil war that ended the Shogunate for good in 'my' Creation) was in full swing when the Great Contagion reached its stride, and between the breakdown of public infrastructure (namely, sewage removal and access to clean drinking water), the lack of trade (namely, not enough food) and the cramming of refugee populations into urban centers, most people werent in tip-top shape.<br /> <br /> It isn't their fault: the Deathlords deliberately released the Great Contagion st the worst possible time in order to have the greatest effect. If the Contagion happened to Creation during the Principate (the early Shogunate, when life was actually pretty nice) or even the Dominate (the late Shogunate, when things were still ok, even if the government got more dictatorial. They made the aqueducts and sewers run, at least), things probably would have worked out okay. Probably between the Spanish Flu and the Black Death.... So, still pretty bad, but not apocalyptic.<br /> <br /> Vectors and Symptoms: the Great Contagion was passed from person to person via body fluids, meaning it ripped through cities like a wildfire. Any drop of fluid from an infected individual could lead to more infections, and there tended to be a LOT of body fluids.<br /> <br /> Picture the unholy lovechild of ebola, influenza, malaria, and diarrhea, and you get a pretty good idea: coughing, sneezing, sweating, vomiting and shitting, all involuntary and all capable of passing along the disease.<br /> <br /> The worst part is is that you can catch the Contagion and not realize it (as in, not show symptoms) for about a week, depending on how strong your immune system is. You can travel around and pass along the all the while..... Which is part pf the reason why people got so xenophobic during the course of the Contagion.<br /> <br /> Generally, the symptoms proceeded like this:<br /> <br /> 1) initial infection. Over the course of a week or so, the infectee will gradually develop quasi-flu-like symptoms, such as runny nose and eyes, headaches and muscle pain, coughing, stomachache, etc. <br /> <br /> 2) as the disease progresses, the infectee develops a fever, which quickly increases in severity. In addition, the infectee suffers increasingly severe nausea and diarrhea, eventually unable to keep down food or water. Most infectees will bleed from the mucus membranes at some point, but this is a byproduct of the irritation to the body than a function of the disease.<br /> <br /> This stage lasts for as long as the patient does, usually only a few days at most, as most people either get fried from the fever or die from dehydration.<br /> <br /> 3) it -is- possible to survive the Great Contagion, albeit extremely difficult. If an infectee manages to break the fever, the other symptoms will fade quickly. Of course, the infectee will need to be administered to in order for this to happen, as most of the time the fever will render them unable to take care of themselves (aka comatose).<br /> <br /> As an aside, many Dragon-Blooded Exalted, since they couldnt die from the disease, tried to help out their mortal subjects the best they were able. Many of the more medically-minded set up hospitals and medical schools, most of which were destroyed in the aftermath of the Contagion, but a few survived to the present day (mainly in the Realm and Lookshy). In addition, the Immaculate Faith (i have the IF as existing in some shape or form for a reeeeaaaallllyyyyy long time) -really- started its charity and aid efforts at this time, and even said that whoever sat at the side of the sick and dying and helped them, the greatest of moral actions, would be boosted up the reincarnation chain.<br /> <br /> Im a fan of making both the Dragon-Blooded and the Immaculate Faith less outwardly puppy-kickingly dickish. To the Immaculate Faith, the title &quot;Prince of the Earth&quot; means that, while you are the top dog, you better goddamn act like it. noblesse oblige is a virtue for the Dragonblooded, at least according to the IF, and the IF isnt afraid to knock dick-dastardly DB's down a peg or two... At least so long as they arent being controlled by the Great Houses.<br /> <br /> Yadda yadda yadda","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-03-31T23:49:52-07:00","id":1201863,"title":"","content":"Glad to see you writing again. As far as character choices go:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Choleric: Associated with yellow bile, the element of fire, the season of summer and the afternoon, you are extroverted, unemotional and task-oriented. You tend to be determined, confident, honest and practical, but also hot-tempered, insensitive, vindictive, and arrogant.</li> <li>Valor: Without bravery, what is the use?</li> <li>The princess of a Shogunate successor-state. Given in treaty to ensure the then-foederati remained loyal, your mother\u2019s name may still carry weight in her homeland (Contact 1: <i>dux Gallia,</i> speak Riverspeak)</li> <li>Axe, the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Francisca\" target=\"_blank\"><i>francisca</i></a>, a light axe popular across most of the North and Northeast, suitable both for hand-to-hand combat and as a throwing weapon. The weapon has a fearsome reputation for causing damage even through armor and helmets (Melee/Thrown specialty: penetrating armor)</li> <li>Training at war and drilling tribal levies: Part of being a ruler, even a barbarian ruler, is that you lead the people in both war and peace. You knew what your role would be, and you prepared for it. While training, you made friends with the sons of other noble families, and using your families name and resources, raised them up as your <i>beaduprat</i> (roughly, brotherhood, similar to the Realm\u2019s Sworn Brotherhood), giving you not only skill at war and arms, but a small group of warriors loyal only to you. (War 2, Melee 2, Presence 2, Command 1 [Size 1 elite battlegroup)</li> <li>I think of this as the &quot;ragnar lothbruck&quot; build. You basically said germanic european invaders and while i should be thinking ostrogoth/visogoth/frankish/jutes all i can conceptualize is vikings. While i like the options other people have given, I'm going with this in particular. The swadians did not survive by asking politely! They crushed those they could and took with impunity. Those that submitted themselves before the swadians were spared and forced to do manual labor! Our gods were hard and stoic, but they made us masters of the world and now is our time to be kings of it!<br /> <br /> &quot;Better To Fight And Fall Than To Live Without Hope&quot;</li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T19:21:13-07:00","id":1202105,"title":"","content":"Chapter 1: The Wheel and the Road<br /> <br /> <br /> <i>\u201cSo I, wandering, Bereft of my homeland, Over these long years, And from there, fully, Went winter-weighted, Over the icy waves, Far from my kinsmen\u2026\u201d</i><br /> <br /> (fragmentary part of the Northern saga-song \u201cThe Wanderer\u201d)<br /> <br /> <u>&gt;Ascending Water, RY-138&lt;</u><br /> <br /> You ride to the crest of the ridgeline, and even the spreading vista of the River of Tears, your destination for the last month, can\u2019t do very much to mitigate your black mood.<br /> <br /> As you turn back to the head of the royal column, you reflect back on what your father has said in the last few weeks, about how the salvation of your people lies on its banks.<br /> <br /> You can\u2019t quite see it, but you share his determination. At the very least, getting down out of the Northern mountain valleys is a top-priority. It is but the first month of autumn, barely out of summer, and yet the northern winds already carry a biting chill, and the upper slopes are thick with frost in the early morning. Pulling the tribes down into the river valley will give you fodder for the livestock, and your people won\u2019t <i>starve</i>, but the winter will be hard nonetheless\u2026..<br /> <br /> Shaking your head, you banish the conjecture from your mind. You have always been confident, determined, but ever since you started taking an active role in the migration of the Swadians, your mind has been plagued by increasing worries. The weight of leadership is increasingly heavy, and you now understand why Father looks so drawn. This <i>is</i> your duty, however, as a prince of your people, and you <i>will</i> take your duty seriously.<br /> <br /> Your face evidently betrays your inner mood, and one of your shield-brothers, Hildebald, knocks you from your reverie by an elbow to the ribs, hard enough to feel it through layers of boiled wool and mail. Shooting him a quick look, he responds,<br /> <br /> \u201cDon\u2019t look so concerned, Rechila! We made it to the River of Tears, in, what, 6 weeks? We will be <i>fine</i>. It isn\u2019t even the middle of autumn, yet!\u201d<br /> <br /> His enthusiasm breaks through and gives you newfound confidence, and you grin, \u201cGood point. We still have a few weeks left before it starts getting <i>actually</i> cold, and that is plenty of time for the wagon-trains to move their lazy asses out of the hills.\u201d<br /> <br /> Another of your shield-brothers, Lugios, chuckles, \u201cI\u2019m not too concerned. The commoners have plenty of fat on them, enough to keep warm. <i>My </i>ass, on the other hand, is worn bare.\u201d<br /> <br /> You interrupt, \u201cOh, shut up. Your squeeze has enough ass for the two of you. Her hips are as wide as a wagon-wheel!\u201d<br /> <br /> He sniffs, \u201cYou are just jealous of Yrsa\u2019s \u2018expansive tracks of land\u2019\u201d, to the roaring laughter of the rest of your <i>beaudprat</i>. The good mood and the banter continues all the way back to the outskirts of the royal pavilion, where one person in particular is waiting to kick the feet out from under your cheer.<br /> <br /> Your younger brother, Adelmar.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhere the hell have you been?\u201d<br /> <br /> Your temper flares at the needling and assumption of authority, but you restrain yourself with some effort. Getting into a pissing contest with your brother won\u2019t solve anything\u2026. Although it would certainly be <i>satisfying</i> to put him in his place publically. Instead, you dismount your horse, taking the moment to compose yourself, then reply with all the dignity a prince of the Swadians can muster,<br /> <br /> \u201cThe King sent me ahead to scout the road, brother. Was there something you needed?\u201d<br /> <br /> Adelmar shifts, rankled at the put-down. Still annoyed, \u201c Not I, but the King has need of us. (He says this last bit with smug pride.) I was to fetch you as soon as I found you.\u201d<br /> <br /> You raise an eyebrow. Him having more work for you isn\u2019t unexpected, but\u2026. Adelmar? He hasn\u2019t been much help, not without a lack of effort on his part. He has a tendency to cock things up, asides from debating in the Thing. What could your father possibly want him to do?<br /> <br /> Shrugging, \u201cVery well. Let us go to the King,\u201d, and after dismissing your retainers, you stride off in the direction of the royal pavilion, Adelmar hot on your heels. As you walk, you idly think about your younger brother, and just why the two of you no longer get along.<br /> <br /> It didn\u2019t used to be this way. When the two of you were younger, you were inseparable, and he worshipped you like one of the heroic ancestors of old. It was only when he entered manhood, and realized that he had a chance, no matter how small, at the Kingship, that your relationship soured\u2026.. and you fully admit, with some regret, that it wasn\u2019t entirely his fault. You were long-used to being in command, and his attempts to bolster his own name (sometimes at your expense, albeit inadvertently), on top of your naturally-dominant personality, led you to have several private disagreements and one very-public argument. It didn\u2019t help that he wasn\u2019t very good at military command nor civilian organization, having drastically messed up both battlefield command and the ordering of several wagon-trains, requiring either you or your father to actively step in and render aid. In the beginning, you tried to gently offer advice, but he evidently took these as criticisms and shut down. Now, the two of you can barely stand to be in the same room as each other, something which, when he isn\u2019t irritating the hell out of you, find to be rather sad.<br /> <br /> You sigh out loud, and he hears it, smirking, \u201cHave a lot on your mind, brother?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c Yes\u201d, bluntly. \u201cThere are still 12,000 people stretched back 5 leagues, almost back to the mountains, and am not sure when they will wander their way down to the plains. The weather is getting colder, and I am worried about the amount of fodder we have for the livestock, in addition to, well, the gods-damned winter on top of that. There are rumors of a blood-feud brewing between the Salians and the Ripuarians, over a <i>fucking cesspit</i>, of all things<i>,</i> and\u2026.. I have fears for the future, as well.\u201d<br /> <br /> Adelmar stays silent, then , \u201cWhat do you mean?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shake your head, alternately amused and annoyed, \u201cWe are moving <i>into</i> a region that our ancestors fled <i>out</i> of, Adelmar. They left for a reason. On top of that, we don\u2019t know what we are walking into. For all we know, the entire Eastern bank of the River of Tears could be a gigantic Shadowland. Or worse.\u201d<br /> <br /> He is silent again, \u201c\u2026. You know, this is the first time in a long time that I\u2019ve seen you show doubt.\u201d<br /> <br /> You stop walking, turning to look him full in the face. Is Adelmar\u2026.. showing concern? Empathizing with you? Or is he just looking for something to get an edge over?<br /> <br /> Firmly but quietly, \u201c<i>I do not doubt</i>, Adelmar. Concern is not doubt. I have every faith in our people\u2019s perseverance, in our father\u2019s plan, and in <i>myself</i>. We <i>will</i> succeed, I am just unsure of how yet.\u201d He doesn\u2019t reply to this, but his face grows sullen. After a moment, you turn and continue walking.<br /> <br /> Once you reach the royal pavilion, two of your father\u2019s retainers hold open the doorflap for you to enter, and you stride into the tent without slowing down. It is sweltering inside, the air heavy with the smells of woodsmoke and wool and flesh, and you unpin the fibula holding your cloak around your shoulders. Handing both your cloak and your helmet to a servant, you and your brother both walk forwards, dropping to a knee respectfully in front of Hermeneric, King of the Swadians.<br /> <br /> He is deep in conversation with the chieftains of the Salians and the Ripuarians, evidently trying to head off any potential blood-feud between the two of them. After a few moments, he notices the two of you kneeling in front of you and rises to his feet.<br /> <br /> \u201cMy noble sons!\u201d Turning to the chieftains, \u201cI have to discuss something of great importance with my sons. May we continue this settlement another time?\u201d<br /> Evidently, the men agreed, and after nodding respectfully to their princes, leave the pavilion quietly. Your father waits until they are gone, then falls back onto his bench with a groan, putting his face in his hands.<br /> <br /> You rise up to your feet, grinning, \u201cWas it as stupid as I heard, father?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c<i>Fuckin\u2019 nonsense</i>!\u201d, he mutters, voice muffled behind his hands. \u201cTwo old, established families, about to go to war because the one family used the other\u2019s cesspit <i>by accident</i> and broke the retaining wall. And, their leaders, instead of sitting down their clan-leaders and smacking some sense into them, egged it on!\u201d He shakes his head, \u201cI need a gods-damned drink.\u201d<br /> <br /> You step up to fix it for him, making one for you and looking over your shoulder at Adelmar, offering a peace treaty. He sourly shakes his head. Shrugging, you bring the mead up to his bench, dragging another over.<br /> <br /> He drains the cup, then turns to you, \u201cSo, how did the scouting go?\u201d<br /> <br /> You shrug, \u201cIt is just as we were told. On the other side of this ridge is the River of Tears.\u201d<br /> <br /> Your father relaxes, something you don\u2019t see him do very often. Resting his head on the tent-pole, he closes his eyes. \u201cDid you see any smoke, any roads, anything that says the land is inhabited?\u201d<br /> <br /> You frown, thinking back, \u201cNo, but I could only see this bank of the river. The other side is too far away to make out any details. I saw some woods, lots of reeds and marshes\u2026.. a couple of villages, but there wasn\u2019t any smoke, so I don\u2019t think they were inhabited.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGood, good.\u201d He stays silent for a long time, and all you hear is the crackling of the fire.<br /> <br /> \u201cI have a very important job, for the two of you.\u201d<br /> <br /> You and your brother lean forward, and he continues, \u201c It is going to take some time before the rest of the wagon-trains make their way out of the hills and into the valley itself. At <i>least</i> four weeks, by my guessing, and probably more. As important is it is that we don\u2019t get caught with our pants down now, it is far more important that we don\u2019t get ambushed on the other side of the river, or, gods forbid, crossing the damn thing\u2026.. which I still don\u2019t know how we are going to do. Build rafts? \u201c<br /> <br /> He is silent again, and Adelmar asks, \u201cYou are asking us to scout the other side of the River of Tears?\u201d It isn\u2019t really a question.<br /> <br /> Your father nods, \u201cTomorrow, I am giving each of you command of part of my retinue. Figure out some way to cross the River, scout around, and try to figure out just what the hell is going on over there.\u201d<br /> <br /> He opens his eyes and sits up, looking at the two of you sternly. \u201cThis is, by far, the most crucial task in the history of our people\u2019s migration. I cannot state this enough: <i>All</i> of our efforts will go to naught if I pile 15,000 people onto shitty rafts, only to find out the entire Eastern half of the River of Tears is a mass-grave, or home to something equally awful.\u201d He sighs, \u201c Each of you will have total command over the mission. No oversight, no advice, and, most importantly \u2026. No relief. Once you cross the river, you will be on your own.\u201d<br /> <br /> He looks at each of you, catching your eyes in turn, \u201cDo you understand?\u201d<br /> <br /> You reply, \u201cI do, Father.\u201d, and Adelmar nods as calmly as he is able.<br /> <br /> \u201cThen go, with my blessing, and do your duty as princes of Swadia. Come here at dawn tomorrow.\u201d With that, he closes his eyes again, deep in thought. After a moment, you stand up, bow respectfully, and leave. Adelmar follows by your heels.<br /> <br /> You leave the pavilion and head back to your own tent, thinking hard about what tomorrow will bring. As night falls, and the wind blows cold off the hillsides, you gather together your <i>beaduprat<u>, </u></i>discussing with them your quickly-made preliminary plans, what you saw from the ridge, as well as what they think the best course of action would be. These plans go on into the night, and you awake bleary-eyed just before dawn with a muttered curse. Getting dressed quickly, you hurry to the royal pavilion.<br /> <br /> Once there, you find about 200 of your father\u2019s retinue loosely assembled, gathering their gear and packing food and other supplies into wagons. It only takes an hour or so to get everything squared away (you are good, but your father has been doing this since before you were born), and you set off with little fanfare. Behind you and your retinue is a warband of 100 or so fighters, leading a wagon loaded with food, although you hope to sustain your scouting foray off the land as much as possible. Your father\u2019s only instructions were to return to the landing site in 6 weeks, and mark said site with a pillar of smoke.<br /> <br /> As you leave, you take care to give your brother a small nod. As sour as your relationship is, he is still your brother, and you wish him success. To your (pleasant) surprise, he actually returns it, although he looks more than a little distressed.<br /> <br /> Almost immediately after cresting the ridge that conceals the royal encampment, your warband starts to descend down into the river valley. Between the two mountain ranges (the ones to your back and the distant ones floating on the horizon), the valley of the River of Tears is broad and flat, with copses of trees and fields of waving grass above the banks, and long, seemingly-never-ending stands of reeds and marshes along the waterside. Upon cursory inspection, the soil appears to be very rich, firm and spongy to the touch and as black as night. You idly wonder out loud to your retainers as to why your people don\u2019t just settle here, on this side of the River, but you already know the answer: your father, and the Althing through him, want a mile-wide river between them and the rest of the North.<br /> <br /> You can certainly understand their desire for safety. The few villages you saw yesterday from the ridge are long-abandoned, with the people either drive off from scavengers (on two legs or four, you don\u2019t know, but this region certainly has a <i>lot</i> of wolves.) or dead from disease. The roofs of the houses are long caved-in and fields and streets are filled with weeds and overgrown grass, and what humans you find are long-dead. Clustered in what look to be religious buildings, or huddled in the corners of homes, they are almost omnipresent. You order your scouts and warriors to dig graves as you scout the area, both as a sign of respect to the dead, but also to prevent them from rising up and ripping your guts out. You have never seen the Restless Dead personally, but your father has told you gruesome stories of encountering them in the past, closer to the Threshold, as well as how to mitigate their potential fury. You aren\u2019t taking any chances.<br /> <br /> While the silence in long-dead villages and fields is uncomfortable, it is almost preferable to the sound of the wind. It moves and breathes like a living thing, moaning through the branches of the trees, whispering through the grass and reeds, and shrieking through the gaping windows and doors of the buildings. Even though you know it is <i>just the wind</i>, it sets your teeth on edge, and it certainly has an effect on the men of your warband. Jumpy and unsettled, they are as eager as you are to find a way to get across the river.<br /> <br /> The one reason you brave the abandoned villages at all is to search for supplies. Not food, you have plenty of that from both what you brought and what you can forage (the land here is unbelievably rich. While the farm-fields and gardens of the villages have long been swallowed up by the forests and grasses, the livestock are roaming wild and seemingly unimpaired by the decline of humanity, asides from the hungry wolves, lynxes and bear. Your foragers bring in feral goat and even cattle with welcome regularity), but supplies for boat/raft building.<br /> <br /> Obviously, asides from a few rare exceptions, most of the boats that did exist in the waterside villages have long been rendered unusable through age or neglect, and the small dinghies wouldn\u2019t carry a warband across the mile-wide River of Tears anyways. So, rafts it is! Now, while the Swadians hail from the \u201cinterior\u201d of the North, away from the coast, and it has been years since you have seen your cousins among the Eridanian river-traders \u2026.. you are a prince, you can manage to build a gods-damned raft. So, your men tear apart the old buildings for the sturdy beams, cut down the conifers for logs, and cut down entire acres of reeds for floatation. Dividing your warband into teams, it takes about a week to scout the immediate riverbank, as well as build enough shoddy rafts to ferry your warband across the river.<br /> <br /> The time has come to push your rafts out into the brackish water of the River of Tears and strike out for the other bank. Standing on the first raft to head out, you take a deep breath and try to calm your nerves, taking care to keep your nervousness hidden from all but your <i>beaduprat.</i> They are nervous as well: Swadians are not a water-going people, more comfortable with both feet on dry land than on a heaving deck. But\u2026.. this is an order and a mission directly from the King, and nobody in the warband would dishonor themselves or their families through cowardice.<br /> <br /> Looking around at your <i>beaduprat<u>, </u></i>you give a nod to Hildebald, who pulls a horn from his belt and lifts it to his lips. Blowing a long blast from it, you listen to it recede into the distance, then give the order to the warriors on the raft to push off.<br /> <br /> Within but a few minutes, you learn (to your great relief) that the rafts you have ordered built are, well, not shitty enough to tip over and send you to a watery grave. However, the seemingly-placid surface of the River of Tears concealed a hidden fury, and the water rushes quickly beneath your feet. In addition, the wind roars across the water, unimpeded by hills or forests. Ultimately, for every quarter-mile you put between you and the initial shore, a full mile whips by upstream ahead of you.<br /> <br /> Well, shit.<br /> <br /> There is nothing you can do asides from paddle, and paddle you do. It takes several hours to paddle across the full width of the river, and that was more helped by the fact that the river-water swirled and ebbed, bringing you close to shore of its own accord. Thankfully, all your rafts got swept up in the same current and by the same winds, and didn\u2019t get <i>too</i> separated. It only takes a few hours to bring your entire warband together, at the mouth of a tributary river feeding into the River of Tears.<br /> <br /> Unfortunately, there is a problem.<br /> <br /> On the banks of this small river, there lie the ruins of an abandoned town, larger than the villages you encountered on the other side of the river. There are a few hours of daylight left, but not enough time to sweep the town for the corpses that are sure to be there, much less dispose of them properly. The land itself is nice and flat, evidently old farmland from the stone walls and old ruts, meaning it will be easy to march through, with hills and forests to the north and south, and mountains rising several miles away to the East.<br /> <br /> Sighing, you think on the best course of action:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Spend the night in the town. You didn\u2019t encounter a single <i>draugr</i> (Skytongue: restless dead, revenant) on the other side of the River, not a single one, so if your Fate holds out, there won't be any here, either. On top of that, your men are tired from the River-crossing, and it might be beneficial to morale to sleep in an actual godsforsaken building for once. It has been years since you have slept with a hard roof over your head\u2026.</li> <li>Push to the north, back to where you originally hoped to land. This was just a little setback, nothing more.</li> <li>Push to the south, where the River decided to take you. The gods work in mysterious ways. Maybe they wanted you to go this way?</li> <li>Something else? (Write in)</li> </ol>OOC:<br /> Since there were only 4 posts deciding Rechila\u2019s makeup, I decided to blend them all together, meaning he will act with a combination of the Choleric and Melancholic characterization, as well as holding the values of Valor (primary) as well as Temperance and Conviction (secondary) . Let\u2019s see how this goes.<br /> <br /> Right now, Rechilia is in command of a Size 3 Battlegroup, stats to come. Most of the warband are light/medium infantry armed with shields, spears, javelins, seaxes, and a few scattered arming coats and helmets. There are also small sections of bowmen and slingers. Plus, of course, Rechila\u2019s <i>beaduprat<u>, </u></i> which is a Size 1 Elite battlegroup of heavy mounted infantry (they ride on horses, but dismount to fight). Asides from that, he has no other cavalry.<br /> <br /> Here is a map of the immediate surroundings, keeping in mind that it is only what Rechila can physically see (his position is marked with a star). If he (you) want to see more of the map, he will need to send scouting parties.<br /> <br /> <img src=\"https://i.imgur.com/873fwPG.png\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-02-2018, 04:48 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T20:02:29-07:00","id":1202111,"title":"","content":"I say make camp in the abandoned town. Say your prayers, put out a circle of grain from your food wagon, but stay here. If this place is a shadowland, your people need to know about it sooner than later. And if it's not... well, people build towns all over, but they grow as big as this one only if they're in a good spot. Premade buildings sturdy enough to stand up after years of neglect could prove useful if winter is coming, and the land and livestock seem bountiful. There might be good fishing or trade to be had here, if it's not haunted this could make a good home base, or at least a good base camp from which to explore the countryside.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T20:29:46-07:00","id":1202115,"title":"","content":"If there's anything dangerous in the town, it would be foolhardy to sleep there. I also don't recommend further exploration when night is coming on. <br /> <br /> I think we should make camp right here, with our backs to the river and the rafts we used to cross within easy reach. Camp now, forage and scout the immediate area, and set up a perimeter. That should be enough to prepare for most potential threats. And if the worst should come to pass, we can get back on our rafts and flee downriver.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T22:06:16-07:00","id":1202134,"title":"","content":"Melancholic.<br /> Willpower.<br /> Shogunate successor princess.<br /> The Spear.<br /> Training at war and drilling tribal levies.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-01T22:08:49-07:00","id":1202136,"title":"","content":"Wait, what town? CHAPTER 1 only has the date arm. I thought we we're still doing the set up of Rechila.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-02T03:27:07-07:00","id":1202179,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1202105#post1202105\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Chapter 1: The Wheel and the Road<br /> <br /> <i>\u201cSo I, wandering, Bereft of my homeland, Over these long years, And from there, fully, Went winter-weighted, Over the icy waves, Far from my kinsmen\u2026\u201d</i><br /> <br /> (fragmentary part of the Northern saga-song \u201cThe Wanderer\u201d)<br /> <br /> [U]&gt;Ascending Water, RY-138</div> </div> </div> </div>I am confused. There used to be more here?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-02T04:04:30-07:00","id":1202185,"title":"","content":"<a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco\" style=\"background-image:url('https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5//avatar1624_2.jpeg');\" class=\"b-bbcode-user b-bbcode-user--has-avatar js-bbcode-user\" data-userid=\"1624\" data-vbnamecard=\"1624\">FallenEco</a> <br /> <br /> The post is back up. Sorry to mess up the formatting!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-02-2018, 04:46 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-03T10:59:15-07:00","id":1202652,"title":"","content":"I'm changing my vote - let's go into town after all. <br /> <br /> Mostly because being cautious doesn't make for as good of a story.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-03T17:56:30-07:00","id":1202817,"title":"","content":"With the onset of the Great Contagion and the broad collapse of urbanized society across Creation, the economies of the survivors may be quite different than what a person living in \u2018modern day\u2019 Creation would expect to see. Below are some of the trade goods one might expect to see across Creation in the aftermath of the Great Contagion.<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Precious Metals: specie, bullion, and other forms</u></i></b><br /> <br /> Before the Interregnum, the Shogunate had a Creation-wide series of specie (in gold and silver and bronze), the value of which was tied to the price of staple agricultural goods and artificially controlled through government purchases of said goods. These valued over the course of their existence, mainly due to climate change between the Principate and the Dominate, until they completely collapsed during the Interregnum.<br /> <br /> The most common of these coins were the silver Denarii, valued at a kilogram of wheat or 3 kilograms of barley at the height of its value (and usually measured as a \u2018standard\u2019 day\u2019s wages for a common laborer), and the bronze Assari, valued at 1/10<sup>th</sup> a Denarii, with multiple denominations. Other values were retained as bullion and used as units of account instead of currency<br /> <br /> During the Interregnum, with the fracturing of the Shogunate Provinces into competing realms and the accompanying breakdown of stable trade and infrastructure, the value of Shogunate specie took a nosedive. Between the different Provinces minting their own, usually disgustingly debased currencies, and the ceasing of controlled values of goods, the coinage of the Interregnum was not worth very much at all, with supplies of grain often taking several times its worth in denarii to purchase, when it was available at all.<br /> <br /> After the Contagion, the value of specie and bullion wasn\u2019t helped by the fact that there was just so much available relative to the surviving population. There was no more governing body to remove excess coinage from the market, nor control the value of the coins.<br /> <br /> To combat this, most survivors do not accept pure coinage as currency, instead relying solely on the weight of the metal as its value. This process, and the accompanying bits and pieces of metal, is commonly called \u2018hacksilver\u201d after the most common examples, and is a common means of exchange in the North to this day, outside of the larger, more centralized states. Traders will measure out specific weights of silver (or other metals), and if a piece of silver weighs too much for the transaction, the trader simply cuts up the piece to get the correct weight.<br /> <br /> Generally speaking, most hacksilver across the North had the following \u201cexchange rates\u201d, keeping in mind that many, if not most, trading was done without seeing a single piece of silver.<br /> Hacksilver was used for large-value trading, between tribes and as tribute, not the small-time exchanging of shiny-bits between neighbors<ul><li>1 ounce of silver (Skytongue: <i>aurar</i>) = approx. 30 or so grams. 1 Shogunate Denarii usually weighed about 3 grams, regardless of the actual silver content</li> <li>8 ounces of silver = 1 ounce of gold</li> <li>8 ounces of silver = 4 milk cows</li> <li>8 ounces of silver = 24 sheep</li> <li>8 ounces of silver = a bolt of wool cloth 72 meters long by 1 meter wide</li> <li>12 ounces of silver = a healthy adult male slave</li> <li>4 ounces of gold = a pattern welded sword</li> </ul>Values were measured in the ounce and the pound, the latter of which is still widely used across the North as a measurement of land-value in the <i>Hide</i>: a hide is the amount of land that will produce goods, almost always agricultural, equaling a pound of silver in value over the course of a year<br /> <br /> (Yep, shit be expensive, especially weapons and armor.. In 8th century England, a coat of mail cost the modern equivalent of about \u00a311,000, while a sword cost about \u00a32,000 for a no-frills blade. A hide of land was worth about \u00a35,000/year.)<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Iron</u></i></b><br /> <br /> Iron is by far the most important metal in human history, and the collapse of society after the Great Contagion is no different. Without iron, there would be no plows to plant grain, sickles to harvest it, nor spears to defend it.<br /> <br /> During the Shogunate, most iron was sourced from shaft-mines, often state-run and staffed by slaves (with hideous costs in human lives) and refined in advanced refineries, resulting in high-quality iron ore, and by proxy tools and weapons, that were <u>relatively</u> inexpensive. After the collapse of the Shogunate, most iron was sourced much more locally, in far smaller amounts, and in much lower qualities.<br /> <br /> In the North, most post-Shogunate iron was sourced from bogs and marshes, which, unlike mines, were sustainable sources, but in very low quantities. In addition, the less-effective bloomeries used to refine said ores were small, meaning finished iron was available only in small quanitites, and took a large amount of labor and time to produce said iron. As a result, most Northern households had, on average, about 100 pounds of iron goods, most of which was tools and cooking equipment.<br /> <br /> Iron weapons and armor were therefore of immense value, and usually passed down from generation to generation until they were no longer usuable. Weapons that used little iron, such as spears and axes, were the most common, while swords, helmets and mail armor were rare and highly sought-after.<br /> <br /> Areas that had lots of bogs and marshes were highly prized as sources of iron ore, and regions where iron mines could be found were, with little exaggeration, worth fighting and dying over.<br /> <br /> In trade, iron was often worth its weight in silver, if not more, due to its direct usefulness. Finished iron bars, axeheads, knife blanks , etc, all were eagerly traded between surviving peoples.<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Salt</u></i></b><br /> <br /> Salt is the most common form of food preservation across Creation, making salt extremely valuable. While smoking, drying, jugging and pickling are all known, none are as simple and as effective as salting.<br /> <br /> That makes salt that much more valuable in the aftermath of the Contagion, and regions where salt can be found that much more worth finding (and by proxy, keeping out of the hands of others.)<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Food</u></i></b>:<br /> <br /> With the breakdown of urban infrastructure and logistics, those who wanted to survive were forced into the countryside and made to learn farming. Foodstuffs are the most common trade-good seen post-Contagion, and even though iron and salt may be worth more due to rarity, foodstuffs are still extremely valuable, arguably the most valuable trade good.<br /> <br /> Everyone has to eat, after all.<br /> <br /> In the North, most foodstuffs take the form of dried grains (wheat, oats, barley, rye, maize), preserved root and leaf vegetables (cabbage, onions, potatoes, turnips, carrots, etc), dried legumes (beans and peas), gourds (pumkpins and squashes) and fruit (most commonly apples, but also pears, cherries, peaches and grapes). The trade in alcohol is also common, as it serves as an effective means of preserving grain or fruit for long periods, as well as making water safe to drink.<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Livestock</u></i></b><br /> <br /> Livestock are valued not just for their meat, but also their milk, their hide and fiber, their horn and bone, and for their labor. The most valuable Northern livestock is the cow, but horses aren\u2019t that far behind, and pigs are the most commonly eaten. The most common by far, however, is the sheep and goat, both of which are often kept in the same pastures.<br /> <br /> The milk from a single cow over a year can keep a family alive through the winter, and the wool from sheep can be easily the most valuable thing a household produces. This is especially important for migrating peoples, since they often do not have the luxury of settling down and putting a crop or two of grain into the ground. Some tribes measure their wealth in the heads of cattle they possess..... and livestock stealing is often a target for young men to raid, eager to attain wealth and status, since the livestock are so valuable on top of being able to walk themselves.<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Cloth and Furs</u></i></b>:<br /> <br /> Nobody wants to be naked, even in the warmer parts of Creation. In the North, wool is the most common cloth seen, followed by linen and nettlecloth and hemp canvas. Cloth is exceedingly labor-intensive to produce (the average weaver can produce about half a square yard of cloth a day, with the thread for that day of weaving usually taking about a day and a half to produce), making it high in value, and in many households, the women that produce the cloth are often the \u2018breadwinners\u2019 of the family, making them indispensable and giving them a great deal of influence. Most cloth is spun using drop-spindles and woven on warp-weighted looms.<br /> <br /> For trading purposes, cloth is measured in <i>ells</i>, a Northern unit of length that roughly corresponds to the length of the forearm, or about 20 inches.<br /> <br /> Asides from cloth, the trade in furs is also brisk. In the frigid North, the pelts of fur-bearing animals are prized for their ability to retain warmth even in the face of the hardest wind. All sorts of pelts are traded, from rabbit to beaver to wolf to bear.<br /> <br /> <b><i><u>Other goods</u></i></b><br /> <br /> Of course, the above are not the only goods traded. Locally-produced goods like lumber, pottery, baskets, etc are valuable.<br /> <br /> Economies:<br /> <br /> With the collapse of the urban, semi-industrialized Shogunate economy, and accompanying cash-money based currency, most surviving groups fell back on barter and gift-based economies. This is especially common in the North, where most tribal groups already used gift-based systems internally before the Contagion, and still use them today. Generally, speaking, barter was used with people external to the group, while gift-giving was used internally.<br /> <br /> When I say \u2018barter\u201d, I really mean trade based on commodity currencies, like what I listed above, instead of a government-backed fiat currency. Silver is <i>generally</i> the medium of exchange, with goods being roughly valued in their worth in silver, but most transactions occur without hacksilver trading hands, only the direct goods.<br /> <br /> Gift-based economies may seem a little weird to the modern observer, so I\u2019m going to try to explain the concept as much as I can, keeping in mind that I am <u>not</u> an economist.<br /> <br /> Let us take, for example, a Swadian clan. The clan-chief is granted a parcel of land, measured in hides (aka, a hide of land is expected to produce goods equal in value to a pound of silver in a year) by the Tribal chief. That, in and of itself, is an example of a gift-based economy: The tribal chief grants land to the clan-chief, in return for assumed support in war and peace.<br /> <br /> Let us go on.<br /> <br /> The clan-chief, in turn, divides out the parcel of land to the subsidiary families of the clan, with the largest parcels generally going to the chief\u2019s primary supporters. In return, the subsidiary families support the chief by supporting the chief in peace and war, and by paying of tribute so the chief can support an armed retinue for their defense.<br /> <br /> In between the families of a clan, there is also gift-giving. Different families may focus on different products and processes. These families make gifts of their produce to each other, so that the clan survives and prospers.<br /> <br /> Example: you have one Swadian family who makes pottery. These pottery pieces are given out to the members of the clan, \u201cfree of charge\u201d, and in return, the potter-family doesn\u2019t have to worry where their food comes from. So on and so forth. <i>Outside</i> of the clan, the pottery will be \u2018sold\u2019 (bartered, for commodities), not given freely.<br /> <br /> Other gifts include the giving of weapons and armor, the fostering of children, the arranging of marriages, the supporting and patronage of skilled labor, and even the giving of loyalty<br /> <br /> For example, let us take Young Dude Swadian, a member of a clan. He is 16, and a man by the standards of his people. According to Swadian law, all men between 16 and 60 <u>must</u> be armed. Since he is of free status, his parents gift to him a seax, the signifier of freedom in the North (seaxes are to be carried <i>everywhere</i>, and to take away someones seax is to make them a slave), but since they can\u2019t afford other weapons, the clan-chief will symbolically present Young Dude with a shield and spear, to signify he is now an adult and ready to take on adult responsibilities within the clan. Young Dude \u2013and- his parents now owe the clan-chief a debt of loyalty for the gift, and may offer some form of tribute in thanks.<br /> <br /> Generally, gifts are intended to be ultimately <u>reciprocal</u>, not one-sided. You don\u2019t have to repay the gift immediately, but it\u2019s expected that the gift is returned at some point. Failing to reciprocate a gift, or even reciprocating with a gift in return that is felt to not be equal in value to the original gift, is a good way to cause a falling-out between the parties, if not an outright blood-feud if the insult is strong enough.<br /> <br /> For example: Young-Dudes mother is known to be a highly-skilled weaver, and she offers to take on the youngest daughter of another family as an apprentice. The other family now owes Young Dude\u2019s mom a gift in return, and to repay the gift, they offer to arrange a marriage between Young Dude and their unmarried cousin, who comes from a poor family and isn\u2019t that skilled. Young Dude\u2019s family is enraged, and, to save face, the other family then offers their eldest daughter in marriage, as well as a parcel of their families land.<br /> <br /> The act of gift-giving can be traced all the way up to the top of Swadian society, and influences most social interactions. Political support for a crucial proposition in the tribal Things or even the Althing are common forms of gift-reciprocation among the Swadian nobility, as well as marriages, the ceding of lands, so on and so forth.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-04-2018, 12:11 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-04T18:15:58-07:00","id":1203217,"title":"","content":"Yeah...we are going into the town, setting up a night watch schedule and if we find any dead, we pray for them and begin preparing funeral rites.<br /> <br /> Edit: And we make sure everyone knows to tell the higher ups if they find someone and what to do.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-05T23:44:50-07:00","id":1203735,"title":"","content":"RULE OF COOL DEMANDS TOWN! RAGNAR LOTHBRUCK DOES NOT FLEE DEATH!","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-06T15:15:23-07:00","id":1204007,"title":"","content":"<div align=\"center\">Northern Warfare: Aims, Methods and Means</div> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> -Evidence of Northern Warfare:<br /> <br /> Most descriptions of warfare in the North come from three broad sources: the saga-songs written by the Northerners themselves, the literary accounts of the Shogunate and Shogunate Successor-States, both military and civilian records, and in physical evidence found in the grave-goods exhumed from warrior-burials found across the North and East.<ul><li>Saga-Songs: these sources, found in both oral histories and in written records (often in the form of runestones commemorating an event), are the most common descriptions of Northern warfare, but also some of the least useful, being often-fragmentary and written in poetic, often euphemistic, verse relying on insular cultural concepts as opposed to more descriptive terms.</li> <li>Shogunate/Post-Shogunate records: Surprisingly, both the Shogunate and the Successor-States neglected to record much in the way of information about the Northern ways of warfare. What few records exist are broadly denigrating, either describing both mass-combat tactics and individual combat styles in forms that, through both physical evidence and the studies of current Northern combat, were discovered to be greatly exaggerated, or, alternatively, describe mainly the aftermath of raids on civilian populations long after the Northern warriors retired from the field.<ul><li>It is important to note, perhaps, that most of these records date close to or after the Interregnum, when the Legionary system was breaking down and largely unable to respond effectively to examples of Northern aggression. Most military records detail Shogunate <i>defeats</i>, with military authors perhaps showing bias and attempting to downplay their own culpability in their defeat, overstating their opponents savagery and underhandedness. Civilian accounts were often taken \u201cafter the fact\u201d, neglecting to consider the effects of trauma on the recollection of such an event.</li> <li>In the few pre-Interregnum records that exist, mainly when the Northern warriors were fighting as allies to the Legions, shows that most Northern cultural groups fought with at least a modicum of discipline. It is unknown if this style of warfare was native to the North, or if Northern Legionary veterans (Northerners made up a surprisingly high number of Legionary recruits) brought the ideas of discipline back with them. They describe their allies as fighting in a shieldwall, as well as being skilled at the charge, albeit the mass-charge, not the individual \u201cscream and run\u201d described in later accounts.</li> </ul></li> <li>Grave-good physical evidence: The most useful forms of evidence, but by far the least common, both the equipment found in burials as well as the condition of any bones that remain often give indications to how the warrior fought in life, as well as how they possibly died. Keep in mind that in many cases, only the rusted remains of iron can be found, with wood, leather, bone and other organic materials long deteriorated.<ul><li>Shields (as indicated by their surviving bosses) appear to have been used by all warriors. Earlier graves seem to suggest that shields were smaller in size, indicating that formations were not so close, while later shields appear to be larger, about 1 meter in width, implying mass-combat was more common. Most shields were round, but in areas heavily influenced by the Shogunate, oval shields are common.</li> <li>Seaxes (that is, the pan-cultural Northern knife) appear in all non-slave graves, men and woman, adult and child. Either as a utility-tool or a weapon of war, it is unknown, but the larger examples would be a handy reserve weapon</li> <li>Spears, both thrusting versions and javelins, were the most common non-seax weapon found, in both noble and commoner graves. It is unknown if these represent weapons of war or hunting tools, but if the North follows the convention seen Creation-wide, it is likely they were both.</li> <li>Axes were the most common hand-weapon. Stereotypically associated with the North, axes were used for both melee and throwing, as evidenced by physical evidence (stained soil or wood residues) in graves. \u201cThrowing\u201d axes exhibit handles around a foot long, \u201cmelee\u201d axes about 2 feet, and axes suited for both somewhere in between. Axeheads are small, single-bitted and tending to have short cutting edges and weighing only about a dozen or so ounces, as opposed to the gigantic double-bitted monstrosities described in post-Shogunate accounts.</li> <li>Swords, helmets, and metal armors (mail is the most commonly found, but lamellar is found in areas influenced by the Shogunate) are exceptionally rare, being found in only approximately 1% of all graves. This indicates that the shield was the primary form of defense for most warriors, at least going on what physical evidence remains.</li> <li>Most injuries appear to occur to the legs, arms and head of warriors, with the shield serving as a very effective defense. Wounds to the torso are rare, and are found mainly in the back, suggesting they occur mainly when a formation is broken and individuals attempt to flee and are cut down.</li> </ul></li> </ul>-Battlefield Tactics<ul><li>The shieldwall: The \u201ctypical\u201d battle involved both sides forming shieldwalls to protect against the launching of missiles, and standing slightly out of range of each other.<ul><li>While many, if not most, Northern saga-songs invoke the sight and sound of shieldwalls crashing into each other, it would appear that, based on the fighting styles of current Northern groups, that each shieldwall would make a large effort to gain as much of a terrain advantage over the other as possible. Many \u201creal world\u201d battles were decided by one side withdrawing once the commander realized they could not push terrain advantage over the opposing force. Common terrain advantages include: holding hilltops, occupying the space <i>below</i> a hilltop, with ranged support on top of the high ground, on opposing sides of a ford or bridge, or with one or both flanks covered by impassible or even only difficult terrain, such as a swamp, a stream , a cliff, etc.<ul><li>As much effort was spent attempting to gain terrain advantage as was spent in a battle. Forced marches were undertaken to secure a strategic ford or ridgeline, or to circumvent the same. Entire wars were won by one side securing a critical terrain advantage over the other, with the disadvantaged side either unable or unwilling to spend lives in effort to dislodge their opponents.</li> </ul></li> <li>Once battle was decided upon, it would appear that most shieldwall-clashes occurred in the following steps:<ul><li>Preliminaries: lines are drawn up and leaders give pre-battle inspirational speeches</li> <li>Advance to close quarters: a battle-cry is given, and one or both shieldwalls advance</li> <li>Exchange of missiles: As the range between shieldwalls close, skirmishers exchange slings-stones, arrows, javelins and axes, in effort to wound or kill opponents and disrupt the formation.<ul><li>Ideally, enough damage was done to the enemy shieldwall through missile attacks, so that any melee combat was merely a \u2018mopping up\u2019 operation instead of a risky back-and-forth. Depending on the ranged weapons used and the discipline and morale of the opponent, this could be common or all-but impossible.</li> <li>It is unknown just how much focus archery was given. Based on the physical evidence found in graves as well as the saga-songs, the javelin was the most common ranged weapon, and, according to the both the saga-songs and witness accounts, melee was generally preferred by the average Northerner. However, bows, being made of wood and horn and sinew, all materials that degrade, probably have not survived the intervening centuries, and there are several culture heroes (including Hjartr Arrow-Odd, who famously killed 20 men with as many arrows in one of the sagas), as well as law-codes that make reference to the use of bows and arrows, evidently expecting the listeners to be familiar with the terminology. It is a worthwhile guess to assume Contagion-Era Northern archery to be similar to modern-day Northern archery, wide-spread, but used for hunting and home-defense as opposed to open warfare in most cases.</li> </ul></li> <li>Shieldwalls connect: one or both sides close the gap and attacks, using shock weapons (spears, swords, knives and axes) and the shield in effort to collapse the enemy line. If the enemy line holds, one or both sides withdraws to rest, and the exchange of missiles continues again.<ul><li>It would appear that there were two main \u201cmethods\u201d to breaking the shieldwall of an opponent, dependent on equipment.<ul><li>If a shieldwall is armed with spears, more space would be left between shieldwalls, to allow for the front row to attack.</li> <li>If shorter weapons are used, shieldwalls will advance until the shields actually touch (described in the saga-songs as a \u2018crash of thunder\u2019, so that swords, axes and seaxes may be used<ul><li>It would appear that both tactics could/would be used at different points in the same battle, depending on the conditions of both shieldwalls and available equipment.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li>Most bouts of close-quarters combat lasted only a few minutes, up to a quarter of an hour at the most. The effort was the break the foes morale and cause them to lose formation and flee, not to kill everyone outright. By fighting in short bouts, the combat effectiveness of your own shieldwall will be kept as high as possible, allowing for a pursuit when the enemy breaks and flees.</li> </ul></li> <li>Rout and pursuit: Eventually, one side would begin to give way, either from wounds, loss of morale (especially with the loss of a leader a warbanner, etc), or exhaustion. A last stand may be made by some, but most will tend to flee. The victors would pursue as much as they were able, killing and capturing as many as they could catch.<ul><li>It is during this routing that it is the most dangerous for the <i>victors<u>. </u></i>Saga-songs tell of victors routing an opposing shieldwall, breaking their own formation in order to chase, only to get crushed by reinforcements moving up or hidden in reserve.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> <li>Individual combat: Most accounts go into great detail with individual combat, due to it being highly visible. They suggest that most individual combat occurred after a warrior left the safety of their own shieldwall to throw missiles.<ul><li>Individual warriors would leave their own shieldwalls in order to gain velocity for javelin and axe throws. This would leave them hideously vulnerable, as they left the cover of their own shieldwall and were fully open to a missile from the opposing side.</li> <li>If a warrior was killed in the \u2018no-mans\u2019land\u2019 between shieldwalls, often someone from the opposing shieldwall would rush out to capture their highly valuable or useful equipment (shield, helmet, sword, extra missiles, etc). The one to do this would in many cases be the one who cast the fatal missile in the first place. Individual or groups of warriors could come to blows between their shieldwalls because of this.</li> <li>Any and all available weapons would be used during these fights, up and including unconventional tactics and improvised weapons (one saga-song recounts the tale of Grettir the Strong, a culture-hero, crushing skulls in an ambush by swinging a cauldron full of soup around by its handle, blinding opponents with the boiling liquid)</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul>-The Horse in War<ul><li>While there are many references to the horse being used in saga-songs, the physical evidence doesn\u2019t bear it out. The Northern breeds of horses are, to this day, largely small and stout, more large ponies as opposed to warhorses, and grave-evidence suggests it was much the same back during the Shogunate.</li> <li>Most Northerners fought as infantry, and while the nobility/elite often rode to war, it would appear that they too dismounted to fight.<ul><li>The horse was mainly used to maneuver the comparatively-heavily armored Northern elite around the battlefield, an arduous task if you have to walk it. On horseback, an elite retainer can quickly shore up a wavering battleline, flank around an enemies shieldwall, or charge a routing foe.</li> </ul></li> <li>A notable exception can be found in the Vlandians, a tribal confederation that moved to the banks of the Yellow River in -174 RY. There, they adopted the larger warhorse of the post-Shogunate successor kingdoms and ended up being very decent shock cavalry.</li> </ul>-Supplying War<ul><li>It is suspected that a good deal of the tribute passed up to tribal chieftains and elites from their sworn underlings was used to support their retained warbands, both in peace and in war.</li> <li>It is known that many/most Northern groups participated in raiding, the sacking of the countryside for food, wealth and slaves. This would allow them to live off the land of the enemy, at least for a while, as well as intimidate the local opposition.</li> <li>Most Northern groups require the ownership of arms by all free peoples (or, alternatively, the ownership of arms means a person is free), and therefore each warrior would be expected to supply their own arms and armor. A chieftain also might gift weapons and armor, especially luxury versions of the same, to retainers in return for their loyalty.</li> </ul>-Aims of War<ul><li>Northerners enacted warfare for many of the same reasons peoples of other Directions waged war, that is: for defense, for vengeance, for loot, for tribute, or for conquest<ul><li>Defense: It is said that \u2018the best defense is a good offense\u201d and one way to prevent yourself from being attacked is to hit them before they hit you. A raid on an enemy may remove their ability to attack you, as well as intimidating them with your strength</li> <li>Vengeance: The need for vengeance is a strong one seen across most cultures of the North, and closely ties in with the concept of honor, both individual and clan-based. If someone wounds you, or your clan, honor dictates you wound them back. No exceptions</li> <li>Loot: Even before the Contagion and its associated climate change, the North was one of the more inhospitable Directions in Creation. The land was poor in many places, and the people looked on the riches of others hungrily. Raiding an enemy and taking their stuff may ensure your family survives the harsh Northern winter.</li> <li>Tribute: Closely related with loot, waging war for tribute is common. Essentially, raiders would attack a region, stopping only when the owners would pay for the raiders to leave them alone.</li> <li>Conquest: Seen during the initial stages of the Contagion, when barbarians migrated across the River of Tears, this is when an entire people marches to war, in order to win more lands.<ul><li>It is important to categorize Northern warfare into two broad groups: raiding and declared war. The main difference is the intended result: booty, in the form of loot, slaves, materials or tribute, or land for settling<ul><li>Raiding is when smaller groups of warriors attack enemies, usually on an unofficial basis. Whether young men banding together to raid a neighboring clans cattle, a clan gathering to enact bloody vengeance, or a tribal chieftain orders an enemies countryside harried in return for tribute, the attacks are usually unsanctioned by the higher leaders of the group (and may be reacted to with a wink and a nudge or outrage, depending on the consequences and relations between the parties). <i>Stopping</i> raids is usually very difficult, as they usually happen without the knowledge of leadership outside of the raiding party, and raids almost always do not constitute war, at least between Northern groups (they are viewed as the natural inclination of young men, who will stop once they get older and settle down, and serve as effective training in arms and combat. Tribal leaders will generally only step in if raiding between groups gets too violent.) Non-Northern groups tend to \u2026\u2026 disagree.</li> <li>Declared war, on the other hand, is when warfare is openly declared by a clan-chief, tribal-chief or King. This, essentially, declares \u2018open season\u201d on the opposing group, and can be <i>very</i> bloody, as the combatants see no need to restrain themselves and prevent undue violence and/or damaged relations. In many cases, \u2018open warfare\u2019 is indicated by an upswing in the occurrence and brutality of raids, as one group seeks to weaken the other before marching in and wiping them out.</li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul></li> </ul>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-06-2018, 07:49 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T17:17:11-07:00","id":1205018,"title":"","content":"You all chose to enter the town. Here we go!<br /> <br /> As your companions gather and order the men of your warband into a cohesive whole, you stand apart, looking across the empty fields and running river to the abandoned town, more lost in thought than actually examining the site. As you turn back to your men, the wind changes direction with the oncoming twilight, shifting from the riverside, losing its tang of salt and taking on something\u2026.. unexpected. You freeze.<br /> <br /> Woodsmoke.<br /> <br /> Heartbeat hammering in your ears, you whip back to the town, eyes straining to catch a glimpse of something, <i>anything</i>, that might be burning\u2026.. there! From the center of town, a small, thind column of white woodsmoke rises into the sky, hidden against the backdrop of the building clouds. You wouldn\u2019t have been able to spot it if you didn\u2019t smell it beforehand.<br /> <br /> Quickly, loud enough to attract the attention of the men closest to you, but not to carry,\u201d Quickly, get off the shore, and drop to the ground!\u201d<br /> <br /> Confused, the men do as they are bid, and your retainers crawl up to you a few minutes later.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat do you see, Rechila?\u201d, hisses Hildebald.<br /> <br /> \u201cSmoke. Woodsmoke, and the cooking of food.\u201d, you reply with relief. \u201cLast I heard, the restless dead don\u2019t cook their meat, so we are dealing with living people, and the town is clear.\u201d<br /> <br /> Fridr, another of your companions, chances a look above the line of waving grass, then ,\u201dOr, it could be a dumbass refugee, that gets eaten as soon as the sun goes down.\u201d<br /> <br /> You think on that, and then shake your head, \u201cI don\u2019t think so. This area has been abandoned for, what, close to a century, and has gods-know how many refugees move through it? Refugees or not, they would know enough to avoid a potential haunting, and that town would be a good place to make camp if it weren\u2019t haunted\u2026\u2026.\u201d<br /> <br /> You think some more, glancing up at the slowly-sinking sun, then call up some of the skirmishers. You bid them to get as close to the town as they can, <i>without</i> being spotted, and try to catch a glimpse of who or what is making that smoke, and to report back before the sun went down. Silently, they slip off, the grass making no sound in their wake.<br /> <br /> The wait is agonizing, and the sun is but a hands-breadth from the horizon (meaning, for those not in the know, there is about an hour of sunlight left. Useful wilderness survival trick), and you are just about to call for a general retreat when two of your scouts suddenly reappear, dragging a heavy burden behind them. As they get closer, the burden is revealed to be the unconscious figure of a man, roped by his ankles. He looks like, well, most other Easterners you\u2019ve seen (not that you have seen many, Easterners being rare these days):,short, skin the yellowish color of the grass surrounding you, black hair and facial stubble. He is dressed in what approximates a\u2026.. military uniform? With a beige canvas tunic underneath a boiled rawhide lamellar vest and helmet. Around his waist is a baldric with an empty dagger sheath, and your skirmishers are carrying the blade, a spear and a shield. These they present to you, and as you look them over, the skirmishers talk of what they saw.<br /> <br /> \u201cWell, warlord, there are <i>definitely</i> people in the town. At least two dozen in the center of town, and sentries around the edges.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHow long until this one is missed, or should we already be moving?\u201d<br /> <br /> The skirmisher grins, \u201cAt least two hours, after dark. They switch off every four, and the shift just changed when we came up on him. The main camp should be finishing up dinner about now.\u201d<br /> <br /> Hidebald frowns, \u201cSingle sentries, in unfamiliar territory? I know a stupid enemy is a gift from the gods, but are they totally incompetent?\u201d<br /> <br /> The scout shrugs, \u201cIt looks so. The main force in the center of town is <i>completely</i> unorganized. It isn\u2019t even fortified! They are holed up inside a hall-looking building instead, and some of the troops were out of armor, playing some sort of ball-game!\u201d He chuckles, and your companions exchange meaningful looks.<br /> <br /> \u201cThat could either be <i>really</i> good, or <i>really</i> bad. We could have either stumbled across completely useless troops\u2026. Or be in the center of their territory where they feel like they can lack picket-discipline.\u201d, you muse.<br /> <br /> Lugios interrupts, \u201cShield-brother, if we were in the center of their territory, I feel that the town would have been rebuilt already, or would be being rebuilt.\u201d He turns to the skirmisher ,\u201d Did you see any workers, any signs of construction?\u201d<br /> <br /> The scout shakes a negative, \u201cNo, my lord. We didn\u2019t see any signs of building. The buildings <i>are</i> in good shape for a Contagion-ruin, but not <i>recent</i> good shape, if you catch my drift, and they don\u2019t look lived in. Grass in the streets, roofs stripped of thatch down to the base level. Only the center-plaza and buildings are maintained.\u201d<br /> <br /> You take that in. \u201cTell me more of these troops.\u201d<br /> <br /> The man shrugs again, then points down to the slowly-awakening man. \u201cThey look like him, pretty much. Probably two dozen sentries around the town, most on the south and east-sides, another two dozen in the center plaza. Most armed like him, spears and shields, a few with bows, but not many. I didn\u2019t see any horses. \u201c<br /> <br /> \u201cOkay, excellent, but\u2026.. why bring him back with you?\u201d gesturing at the bound sentry.<br /> <br /> \u201cOrdinarily, I wouldn\u2019t warlord, but when we snuck up on him and dragged him into a bush, he must have heard us speaking Swadian, because he switched from whatever gibberish he was trying to talk in to <i>Eridanian</i>. Its awful, and I only got a word in ten, but he speaks our tongue, after a fashion. Thought you might want to talk to him.\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cGood. Good.\u201d You turn your attention to the writhing man at your feet. \u201cDrag him down to the riverbank, behind the strand of reeds.\u201d<br /> <br /> There, you order him stripped, then cold, salty riverwater dumped on his head. It takes but a single helmet-full to bring him to full awareness, and his shout of surprise is only stopped short by your foot pressed firmly onto his chest, forcing the air from his lungs. His eyes open as wide as saucers, even more so once you draw your sword and put the point under his chin. You let him sweat there a moment, in order to recollect just how to speak Eridanian.<br /> <br /> (The Eridanians are a Northern people , sorta like the Kievan Rus, at least in ancestry, and Eridanian is largely based off Skytongue. There are several loanwords and inflections from both Low Realm [Low Realm proper doesn\u2019t <i>really</i> exist yet, its still largely a dialect of High Realm) and Dragontongue [Dragontongue was the Low/High Realm of the East, a Greek to the Latin if you will, and died out to the Contagion], meaning Rechila has to parse out what he says, especially since it has been some time since he spoke it. The difference between Swadian-Skytongue and Eridanian-Skytongue is sorta like English and German, if German has more Greek influences, for those interested.)<br /> <br /> \u201cSo.\u201d You start, looking him full in the face. \u201cI have been told you speak Eridanian. If so, nod if you can understand me.\u201d<br /> <br /> After a second, he nods, careful of the blade prickling his throat.<br /> <br /> \u201cGood\u201d, removing the blade and lessening up the pressure on his chest. \u201cI don\u2019t have to tell you what will happen if you get <i>too</i> obnoxious, correct\u201d?<br /> <br /> \u201cO-of course, my Lord.\u201d He stumbles over the words, his fear not helping his thick accent.<br /> <br /> \u201cWonderful. Now\u2026. You are going to tell me <i>everything</i> about what is going on in that town. Where you are from, who sent you, how many warriors you have, and, most importantly, <i>why you are here</i>.\u201d<br /> <br /> And that was all it took for him to spill everything.<br /> <br /> Apparently:<ul><li>The mans name was Artemas, and he was a soldier for the Shogunate successor-state of Turontum</li> <li>Turontum was a week and a half\u2019s march to the East, beyond the mountains and on the banks of the Durian River</li> <li>There were 4 <i>contubernium</i>, or about 40 fighters, in the town, and they were there to watch on the movements of several large refugee camps located at the mouth of the Durian</li> <li>River, a few miles to the south. If the refugees moved en masse, warning was to be sent to the main <i>Cohort </i>in Turontum proper by messenger pigeon.</li> <li>Discipline was lax. <i>Cohort I Occidentalis</i> was, by all accounts, the smallest (half-strength), underappreciated (away from the main threats) and underequipped division of the Turontum Legion. This migrant-watching posting was viewed as a punishment-detail, and both the soldiers and the commander didn\u2019t really give a damn.</li> <li>The commander of the post, <i>Optio</i> Vegetius Renatus, was a young, puffed-up noble from the capital, chosen for the rank not for his skills but for his connections. He mainly walked around shouting useless orders that nobody followed anyways, or stayed in his office. Other than that, the soldiers were largely left to themselves.</li> </ul>The man stammers his way through his description, then waits silently as you pace back and forth on the beach, cringing whenever you or another of your men look his way. Suddenly, he bursts out,<br /> <br /> \u201cForgive me, my Lord, but\u2026\u2026 there may be a way we both profit from this!\u201d<br /> <br /> As your men curse and put their weapons away, looking uneasily at the town, you walk up to him. Towering over him, lying on the ground as he is, you ask, in a quiet voice.<br /> <br /> \u201cWhat do you mean, profit?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cW-well, there are the refugees to the south of us, and the whole reason we are here is to keep an eye on their movements and prevent them from moving into Turontum proper. I-If you\u2026..if you were to take out these refugees, we would be able to go home, and I am sure you would be paid very well.\u201d<br /> <br /> You raise an eyebrow, \u201cYou have enough <i>something</i> in that abandoned settlement to be worth my mens while?\u201d<br /> <br /> He freezes, realizing the implications, \u201cN-No, my Lord. You would have to go see the Dux himself, at the <i>Castra Sinica</i>, but\u2026. he has issued public proclamations of the threat refugees and migrants portray to the settled realm, so I am sure he would be willing to offer whatever sort of payment you want. Money, food, land! Gods only know there is enough land now\u2026. Hells, he might even offer you a long-term contract, to fight off the Khergit raiders!\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201c\u2026.Khergit raiders? Who are they?\u201d<br /> <br /> \u201cHorse nomads, Lord. They live to the East, and the South, and they raid anywhere their horses take them. We try to bargain with them ,pay them in silver and cattle and iron, but they just keep coming. There are a line of border-forts, along the eastern edge of Turontum, but they just slip around, to the south and the north, and nowhere in Turontum is safe! In fact, the <i>Dux</i> has a bounty on Khergit scalps, an ounce of silver a head. Easy money!\u201d<br /> <br /> You don\u2019t reply, stepping back from the man to look out over the grass. You <i>could</i> do something about these migrants for these pretend-Shogunate fools, get in their good graces so that when your people arrive, they meet friends and not potential enemies. On the other hand\u2026.. you doubt they would be happy to hear about almost 15,000 people arriving on their western shore. On the <i>other </i>other hand\u2026\u2026 it really sounds like these people already have their hands full, and your people could carve out their own territory without too much issue. However, the Khergit nomads sound like they could be a problem.<br /> <br /> You:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Offer to meet with the commander, to talk terms about doing something about the migrant-camps. \u201cAs the saying goes, take me to your leader!\u201d</li> <li>Move to take out the post. You don\u2019t want anyone knowing your people are going to arrive soon , much less are already here.</li> <li>Leave. You have too much to do to get caught up in local squabbles. You will just have to find a different place for your people to land.</li> <li>Something else? (write in)</li> </ol>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":11892,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","user":"Boston123","date":". 04-09-2018, 10:16 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T18:58:29-07:00","id":1205044,"title":"","content":"<img src=\"https://media.giphy.com/media/TO8FXGU7qq9Rm/giphy.gif\" class=\"bbcode-attachment bbcode-attachment--lightbox js-lightbox\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" itemprop=\"image\" loading=\"lazy\"/><br /> <br /> Option number 2. Also someone has been playing waaaaay too much mount and blade.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8897,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","user":"joshopotamus","date":". 04-09-2018, 10:01 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T19:12:34-07:00","id":1205048,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>joshopotamus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1205044#post1205044\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Option number 2. Also someone has been playing waaaaay too much mount and blade.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Most of my campaigns are based off of some combination of a Total War campaign or Mount and Blade. Soooo..... yes.<br /> <br /> But, then again, you can't get too much of a good thing.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-09T22:51:22-07:00","id":1205103,"title":"","content":"Take the post, maybe send a messenger to the migrants as a bonus. Not feeling the love for these 'Shogunates' but the refugees they are oppressing could be useful.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-10T00:27:19-07:00","id":1205117,"title":"","content":"I see this is why i enjoy your quests. I love both of those games with all of my heart. No you cant get too much of either. I also cant stop using that conan gif","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8897-joshopotamus","name":"joshopotamus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar8897_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"97"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-13T16:27:45-07:00","id":1206718,"title":"","content":"Take the post","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11567-jairain","name":"Jairain","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11567_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"345"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-13T20:52:10-07:00","id":1206781,"title":"","content":"Yeeeahhh I vote for murder these dudes. Anybody who's happy to pay strange foreign warriors to massacre a bunch of refugees is an adder waiting to bite. Refugees could be a problem or a resource.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>So I'm making <a href=\"http://godkickingboot.smackjeeves.com/\" target=\"_blank\">God-Kicking Boot</a>, an Exalted webcomic, now. Updates on Sundays. Full-color, mediocre but slowly improving art. It's a thing.<br /> <br /> The absence of a monument can, in its own way, be something of a monument also.</i> -Roger Zelazny","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/512-wise-old-guru","name":"Wise Old Guru","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar512_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1097"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-13T21:01:21-07:00","id":1206783,"title":"","content":"Attack the post. Kill any who resist, bind those who surrender into service.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>On the frontier of the Wild South, there's only one woman with the grit to take on its most dangerous outlaws and bring them <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/984977-back-alive-or-maybe-dead-quest\" target=\"_blank\">Back Alive, or Maybe Dead.</a><br /> <br /> Avatar by <a href=\"http://puzzlinghappenstance.tumblr.com/\" target=\"_blank\">K.S. Brenowitz</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/908-semicasual","name":"semicasual","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar908_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2499"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-15T12:45:51-07:00","id":1207373,"title":"","content":"No post today, Im working overnight until friday.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-18T06:20:30-07:00","id":1208416,"title":"","content":"I have to say it's delightful that you've thought about not only the history but the historiography of your setting. <br /> <br /> I'm late to the party and the vote seems unanimous in favour of my preferred choice anyway, but I'll throw in my 2c and vote for <b>taking the post</b>. Turontum might not mind having a warband of barbarians on its payroll, but they'll feel differently about 15,000 of them descending on the land they're trying to claim, especially given how they feel about migrants. I don't see that offer leading to a stable alliance. Maybe the Swadians can continue to make themselves useful by taking on the Khergit, but they'll always be junior partners with the government of Turontum unless they assert themselves, which is likely to end in a fight anyway. Also, screw this guy. He admits that there's plenty of land to go around but they don't want the refugees to have any of it? Put them to the sword! We're more likely to find common cause with the people antagonising them, anyway. <br /> <br /> As an aside, what the demonym for Turontum?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>&quot;Measure of Hope is right about everything.&quot; - Wise Old Guru<br /> <br /> Currently running an Exalted 2.5 Abyssals game in a homebrew modern shard because I value neither my time or my sanity, and I'm loving almost every minute of it.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/23907-measure-of-hope","name":"Measure of Hope","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2018"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"190"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-18T15:24:20-07:00","id":1208598,"title":"","content":"1) Most people in post-Contagion Creation dislike refugees. Hell, the Swadians dislike refugees, and they are migrants themselves.<br /> <br /> The main reason for this dislike is xenophobia. Even if they speak the same language as you (which isn't likely), chances are the refugees are going to be fleeing the urban centers of the inner Threshold and Yellow River Valley, meaning they don't exactly have much in the way of practical skills for the now-superrural surviving groups, and therefore they can't be easily folded into the developing societies.<br /> <br /> On the other hand, it isn't all that uncommon for refugee groups to be taken into survivor-states as a form of underclass, pseudo-serfdom at best and outright slavery at worst, so they can be productive through sheer weight of mass. The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Latifundium\" target=\"_blank\"><i>latifundium</i></a> can always use more workers... <br /> <br /> Another reason why refugees are so disliked is disease. Even if they don't bring the Contagion with them, they often carry other contagious diseases that can wipe out entire areas, if not with them when they move, then when they set up camp. OOC: the refugee camps to the south this soldier is talking about are infested with cholera, which is why there are Turontum soliders there to watch their movements in the first place. The <i>Ducatus</i> doesn't want cholera moving into Turontum if it can be prevented, especially when he has the Khergit breathing down his neck.<br /> <br /> They also have a tendency to pick areas clean of firewood and huntable game, tear up fields, befoul the water with shit, and other messes associated with large groups of people even today. It isn't a big deal when nobody else lives on the land they put camps on, but when they start squatting on farm fields and the like.....<br /> <br /> Of course, none of this is <i>really</i> their fault, they are living through the apocalypse, after all. It is just that the other survivors can't spend too much mercy.<br /> <br /> 2) The demonym of Turontum is Turontan, although the state doesn't really have much in the way of a 'national identity&quot;. People will usually identify themselves based on what <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gens\" target=\"_blank\"><i>gens</i></a> they belong to, then what village or region they come from, <i>then</i> as a Turontan.<br /> <br /> 3) The <i>Natio Turontum</i> actually claims a huuuuuge amount of land. While it only controls a small portion of this <i>de facto</i>, it claims a large stretch up and down the banks of the River of Tears <i>de jure</i>..... including pretty much all the potential settling sites for the Swadians. Of course, the only ones who actually recognize these claims as legitimate are, well, the Turontans, and asides from moving troops (which the <i>Ducatus</i> can't spare, asides from conscripting more levies, which he can't really do) and settlers (which he also can't do, as he just doesn't have the population) to actually enforce the claim, there isn't much he can do if someone decides to settle in that area. Hundreds of square miles of mountains, rivers, fields and forests, bursting with available resources, and he can't do anything except look at it in his minds eye.<br /> <br /> &quot;Water, water everywhere, and not a drop to drink&quot;.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-24T16:03:36-07:00","id":1211198,"title":"","content":"Sorry, folks, i meant to get the post out today, but work kicked me in the teeth.<br /> <br /> Tomorrow","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11892-boston123","name":"Boston123","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2016"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"790"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2018-04-25T04:53:15-07:00","id":1211363,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Boston123</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/1199787-the-time-of-salt-and-iron-quest?p=1211198#post1211198\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Sorry, folks, i meant to get the post out today, but work kicked me in the teeth.<br /> <br /> Tomorrow</div> </div> </div> </div>It is okay. Thank you for another Quest.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Thoughts ripple out, birthing others","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1624-falleneco","name":"FallenEco","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1624_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1927"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}